{"name":"Prunella","hex":"#864788","fetched":"2026-10-16","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"Prunella","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Proper noun","definitions":["A taxonomic genus within the family Prunellidae – accentors.","A taxonomic genus within the family Lamiaceae – selfheal."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/Prunella"}],"entries":[{"title":"Prunella (fairy tale)","blocks":[{"text":"\"Prunella\" is an Italian fairy tale, originally known as Prezzemolina. Andrew Lang included it in The Grey Fairy Book. It is Aarne-Thompson type 310, the Maiden in the Tower."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Prunella_(fairy_tale)","wikidata":"Q7253039"},{"title":"Prunella (cloth)","blocks":[{"text":"In clothing, prunella is a worsted fabric, sometimes also made with a blend of silk. Documented from the 17th to the 20th centuries,\nit was produced in Norwich.\nPrunella was used in a variety of garments such as petticoats, academic and barristers' gowns, judges' robes, coats, waistcoats, and shoes."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Prunella_(cloth)","wikidata":"Q107121195"},{"title":"Prunella (plant)","blocks":[{"text":"Prunella is a genus of herbaceous plants in the family Lamiaceae, also known as self-heals, heal-all, or allheal for their use in herbal medicine."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/cb/Prunella_vulgaris_-_harilik_k%C3%A4bihein.jpg/330px-Prunella_vulgaris_-_harilik_k%C3%A4bihein.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Prunella_(plant)","wikidata":"Q148652"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f0/Prezzemolina_opens_the_Scatola_del_Bel-Giullare_-_title.jpg/330px-Prezzemolina_opens_the_Scatola_del_Bel-Giullare_-_title.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f0/Prezzemolina_opens_the_Scatola_del_Bel-Giullare_-_t